<h2>What Wix genuinely does well</h2>
<p>Like our <a href="/guides/facebook-page-vs-website-for-tradies/">Facebook comparison</a>, this isn't a hit job. Wix earns its place for some people:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>It's real and it works.</strong> Modern builder sites look fine, load fine, and — contrary to a myth agencies love — can rank on Google. The platform isn't the weakness.</li>
  <li><strong>Cheap to start.</strong> A few hundred a year, no big upfront hit. For a side hustle or a brand-new solo operation testing the waters, that's rational.</li>
  <li><strong>Total control.</strong> Change a photo at midnight without emailing anyone. Some people genuinely enjoy this. If that's you, and you'll actually maintain it, go well.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Now do the real maths</h2>
<p>Here's the part the "from $16/month" ads leave out. A website that brings in work is five-plus pages: a homepage, your services, your area, proof you're legit, and a way to contact you — each one laid out, written, and pointed at the searches people actually type.</p>
<p>Doing that competently for the first time is <strong>30–60 hours</strong>. Not of watching TV with a laptop — of writing copy, resizing photos, fighting templates, and googling "what is a meta description".</p>
<p>You already know what an hour of skilled labour costs, because you sell them. At a charge-out rate of NZ$90–$120:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>DIY on Wix</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Subscription, year one</td><td>NZ$276–$780</td></tr>
      <tr><td>40 hours of your time at NZ$90–$120/hr</td><td><strong>NZ$3,600–$4,800</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Real year-one cost</td><td><strong>NZ$3,900–$5,600</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>And that assumes it gets finished. The most common DIY outcome we see isn't a bad website — it's a 40%-built one that's been "nearly done" since summer, quietly costing jobs the whole time. (Whether you need one at all is a fair question — <a href="/guides/do-tradies-need-a-website/">we've answered it honestly here</a>.)</p>

<h2>When DIY is the right call</h2>
<p>Straight up, because pretending otherwise would insult your intelligence:</p>
<ul>
  <li>You're testing whether the business is even a thing — don't spend real money yet.</li>
  <li>Work is a hobby or a side gig, and the website is too.</li>
  <li>You actually enjoy this stuff and treat the hours as a hobby, not labour.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When done-for-you is the right call</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Your hours are billable and the backlog is real — the site's opportunity cost is quoted work you didn't do.</li>
  <li>You want to be found on Google, not just have a link for your business card. The SEO and copywriting are the hard part, and they're exactly what DIY skips.</li>
  <li>You want it <em>finished</em> — live by a date someone else is accountable for.</li>
</ul>
